  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nDaily potential for patchy valley fog will be the main theme as\nfar as hazards are concerned. Fog will affect some aviation and\ndriving late at night through the early morning hours along \nriver valleys and deep hollers. Boundary layer winds will likely\nweaken on a daily trend with decoupling at the surface to allow\nfor clam surface flow. Meanwhile, relatively low and mid cloud \nfree decks on a daily basis along with radiational cooling \ndropping temperatures to their dewpoints every night will allow \nfor the valley fog to potentially form for certain river valleys\ndepending on the environmental setup each night. \n\nAn abundant amount of dense fog is out of the question as far \nas impacts are concerned and only temporary conditions of patchy\nfog will likely become apparent under the right circumstances. \nAny fog should dissipate by mid morning each day. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA high pressure blocking pattern will prevail into the short \nterm and even long term periods providing pleasant and quiet \nweather outside of some minor instances of potential shower or \nthunderstorm activity for Tuesday and possibly Wednesday.\n\nA small disturbance will have the potential to bring showers \nto the area from Sunday evening through Monday afternoon. This \nwill be confined to the southern portions of the county warning.\nWe cannot rule out the potential for a storm or two during peak\nheating hours toward the southern coal fields. This will \ninclude our southwestern VA counties as well which will have the\ngreatest probability of any convection.\n\nAnother larger disturbance, a possible cut-off low pressure\nsystem coming in from the north, is forecast to setup further \neast of the area by midweek. This feature has chances of rain \nand/or thunderstorm potential which could potentially cause \nimpacts under thunderstorm activity to the northeast mountains \nand along the mountains south of there on Tuesday afternoon and\npossibly part of the first half of Wednesday as the area will \nbe under the influence of an upper level trough. High pressure \nwill then keep the area settled the rest of the work week. The \nnext chance of rain will likely be on the weekend, but there is\na lot of uncertainty in the models as of now trying to decide \nhow long upper level ridging will hold in place.\n\nImpacts to the rest of the area will remain fairly quiet amid \nanother possible blocking high pressure system which will\npromote the lack of any rainfall chances for the rest of the\nwork week. Temperatures will still remain fairly seasonable and\npleasant for the short and long term forecast periods with only\nminimal impacts expected from fog or possible sub-severe \nthunderstorm activity.",
